[]($section.id('Intro'))

I've left a lot of tabs open about implementing hot reloading for gameplay code.
It's not that I don't know how to do it; I've done it enough times that I don't need to keep these guides around.
I guess I've just been dissatisfied with how I've implemented it in the past,
and keeping those guides open was a sort of way to remind myself to keep thinking about approaches.

There are parts of my implementation that can definitely be made more ergonomic,
but I'm realizing now that a lot of my annoyance with it is kind of just the cost of doing buisness.
Sometimes, when you're programming things from scratch, stuff is just gonna be annoying.

So for this post, I'm gonna look through one of my (most recently abandoned) game projects,
how I wrote the hot reloading for it, and how I would do it today.

## [Basics]($section.id('Basics'))

When you're making a game project that doesn't have hot reloading, the main-loop probably looks something like this:

```c
/* main.c */

void game_init(void)   { /* ... */ }
void game_update(void) { /* ... */ }
void game_deinit(void) { /* ... */ }

int main(void) {
    // ---- Init ----
    game_init();

    while (!should_quit) {

        // ---- Update ----
        game_update();
    }

    // ---- Clean up ----
    game_deinit();
}
```

The basic idea of hot reloading is that the game's functions (`game_init/update/deinit`)
are located in a seperate module that is found and loaded at runtime.
To reload the game code without stopping the whole program,
you just unload the module, recompile it, load the new one,
and now you have freshly modified versions of `init/update/deinit` ready to call.

First, the game functions need to be moved to a shared library and be exported to be used from the main executable.

```c
/* game.c */

extern void game_init(void)   { /* ... */ }
extern void game_update(void) { /* ... */ }
extern void game_deinit(void) { /* ... */ }
```

Then, when this file is compiled into its own dynamic library,
the main executable can use use OS APIs to find the functions it exports.

```c
/* main.c */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <windows.h> // For library loading functions

static HMODULE game_module = INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE;

typedef void (*GameInitFunction)   (void);
typedef void (*GameUpdateFunction) (void);
typedef void (*GameDeinitFunction) (void);

// Pointers to the game functions loaded from the game module
GameInitFunction   game_init    = NULL;
GameUpdateFunction game_update  = NULL;
GameDeinitFunction game_deinit = NULL;

void reload_game_functions(void) {
    // Unload the old library
    FreeLibrary(game_module);

    FILE *game_dll = fopen("game.dll", "rb");
    FILE *game_loaded_dll = fopen("game_loaded.dll", "w");

    // Copy the library to another file so that you don't run into
    // issues with the file still being in-use when recompiling
    while ((character = fgetc(source_file)) != EOF) {
        fputc(character, destination_file);
    }

    fclose(game_dll);
    fclose(game_loaded_dll);

    // Load the new library from the copied file
    game_module = LoadLibraryA("game_loaded.dll");

    // Get the function pointers from the new library
    game_init   = (GameInitfunction)   GetProcAddress(game_module, "game_init");
    game_update = (GameUpdatefunction) GetProcAddress(game_module, "game_update");
    game_deinit = (GameDeinitfunction) GetProcAddress(game_module, "game_deinit");
}
```

Now, if `reload_game_functions` is called before any of the game functions are called,
everything should run exactly like before.

The simplest way to trigger a reload whenever game.dll gets recompiled is by checking if the file was modified at any point after the last time is was reloaded.

```c
/* main.c */

FILETIME last_reload = 0;

bool game_should_reload(HANDLE dll, FILETIME *last_write) {
    // Read the dll's time-related properties.
    // We only care about `last_write` though
    FILETIME _creation_time, _last_access;
    GetFileTime(dll, &_creation_time, &_last_access, last_write);

    // returns 1 if the first arg is a later point in time than the second one,
    // which means a reload should happen
    return CompareFileTime(last_write, &last_reload) == 1;
}
```

The actual "reloading" part happens inside the main loop itself.

```c
/* main.c */

int main(void) {

    // ---- Init ----

    // Do the initial load
    reload_game_functions();

    // Set the last reload time to the current time and
    GetSystemTimeAsFileTime(&last_reload);

    // Open game.dll to read its properties
    HANDLE game_dll = CreateFile(
        "game.dll",
        GENERIC_READ, FILE_SHARE_READ, NULL,
        OPEN_EXISTING, FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL, NULL
    );

    game_init();

    while (!should_quit) {
        // ---- Reloading ----

        FILETIME last_write;
        if (game_should_reload(game_dll, &last_write)) {
            reload_game_functions();

            // Update the last reload time
            // so that another reload won't happen until game.dll is written to again
            last_reload = last_write;
        }

        // ---- Update ----

        game_update();
    }

    // ---- Clean up ----

    game_deinit();

    CloseHandle(game_dll);
    FreeLibrary(game_module);
}
```

In this specific project of mine, I was using [Make]($link.url('https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Make_(software)').new(true)) as a build system.

To run a build of the entire project (ex: `main.c` and `game.c` together), I would just run `make`,
but if the program was running and I wanted build only `game.c` and trigger a hot reload, I would run `make game`,
where `game` is a phony rule.

Roughly, the rules were like this:

```make
.PHONY: all
all: engine

.PHONY: game
game: $(OUT_DIR)/game.dll

$(OUT_DIR)/engine.exe: main.c game.c
	$(CC) $(INCLUDES) -o $(OUT_DIR)/engine $< $(C_FLAGS) $(LINK_FLAGS) -lopengl32 -lgdi32

$(OUT_DIR)/game.dll: game.c
	$(CC) $(INCLUDES) -shared -o $(OUT_DIR)/game.dll $< $(C_FLAGS) $(LINK_FLAGS) -lopengl32 -lgdi32
```

## [Persistent State]($section.id('Persistent-State'))

That part handles the reloading of the actual game code without the window closing,
but assuming game-state is kept in file-scoped variables in `game.c`,
their values are going to get reinitialized on every reload,
so its not much different that just restarting the program.
Worse is, if anything is being done inside of `game_init`,
those changes are effectively erased when the reload happens,
and init isn't going to run again because its not supposed to.

A solution to this is to give ownership of the game state to the main executable so the data lives between reloads.

The way I did it in this project is by defining te game's state struct in its header.

```c
/* game.h */

struct State {
    // ---- Engine references ----

    struct Renderer *renderer;
    struct Input *input;

    // ---- Game state ----
    bool tilemap[TILEMAP_WIDTH][TILEMAP_HEIGHT];

    struct Vector2 player_position;
    struct Vector2 player_velocity;
    struct Vector2 player_acceleration;
};
```

Then, in the main function, the state is allocated on the stack,
and all the game functions are passed a pointer to it.

```c
/* main.c */

#include "game.h"

int main(void) {

    // ---- Init ----
    // ...

    struct State game_state = {
        .renderer = &renderer,
        .input    = &input,
    };
    
    game_init(&game_state);

    while (!should_quit) {
        // ...

        // ---- Update ----

        game_update(&game_state);
    }

    // ---- Clean up ----

    game_deinit(&game_state);

    // ...
}
```

I don't know why I thought this was a good idea. Maybe I was just really into using stack memory at the time.

Creating the state on the stack requires the struct to be publically visible,
which requires a header file.
And having a header file is not the end of the world,
but It does introduce a bit of jumping around which can get annoying pretty quickly.

By allocating the game state on the heap,
and giving the game code responsibility of that allocation, the header file no longer needs to exist.


```c
/* game.c */

// Moved from the header into the source file 
// because it doesnt need to be publically visible anymore
struct State {
    // ---- Engine references ----

    struct Renderer *renderer;
    struct Input *input;

    // ---- Game state ----
    bool tilemap[TILEMAP_WIDTH][TILEMAP_HEIGHT];

    struct Vector2 player_position;
    struct Vector2 player_velocity;
    struct Vector2 player_acceleration;
};

// This is a pointer because the state needs to exist after the DLL is unloaded
struct State *self = NULL;

void game_init(struct Renderer *renderer, struct Input *input) {
    // Heap allocate a new game state
    self = malloc(sizeof(struct State));
    memset(self, 0, sizeof(struct State));

    self.renderer = renderer;
    self.input    = input;
}

// This is a new API that the engine code will use to save the state in order to pass to the reloaded DLL
void *game_memory(void) {
    return self;
}

// This is a new API that the engine code will use to give the reloaded DLL the state from before the reload
void game_reload(void *state_ptr) {
    self = (struct State*) state_ptr;
}

void game_deinit(void) {
    // Now we need to deallocate the state when we're done with it because it was heap allocated
    free(self);
}
```


```c
/* main.c */

typedef void *(*GameMemoryFunction) (void); // NEW
typedef void  (*GameReloadFunction) (void *); // NEW
typedef void  (*GameInitFunction)   (struct Renderer *, struct Input *); // CHANGED

// Of course the new functions also need to be loaded inside of `reload_game_functions`
```

```c
/* main.c */
/* Inside the main loop */

// ---- Reloading ----

FILETIME last_write;
if (game_should_reload(game_dll, &last_write)) {
     // Save the state before reloading
    void *game_state = game_memory();

    // Reload
    reload_game_functions();

    // Give the old state to the newly loaded DLL
    game_reload(game_state);

    last_reload = last_write;
}
```

## [Allocator Objects]($section.id('Allocator-Objects'))

In this project, I was using a collection of custom allocators with API's designed very similar to [those found in Zig's standard library]($link.url('https://zig.guide/master/standard-library/allocators/').new(true)).

The example from before, with the stack allocated game state looks more like this in the actual codebase:

```c
/* game.h */

struct State {
    // ---- Engine references ----

    struct Renderer *renderer;
    struct Input *input;

    struct Allocator *persistent_allocator;
    struct Allocator *frame_allocator;

    // ...
};
```

```c
/* main.c */

int main(void) {

    // ...
    
    struct ArenaAllocator persistent = arena_allocator_init(MiB(10));
    struct ArenaAllocator frame      = arena_allocator_init(MiB(10));

    struct State game_state = {
        .renderer = &renderer,
        .input    = &input,

        .persistent_allocator = &persistent.allocator,
        .frame_allocator      = &persistent.allocator,
    };
    
    game_init(&game_state);

    while (!should_quit) {
        // ...

        game_update(&game_state);
        arena_clear(frame_allocator);
    }

    game_deinit(&game_state);

    // ...
}
```

A problem that I got really hung up on when I was writing this project originally was how to organize the use of these allocators across the seperation of engine and game code.



